{"rank": 68, "article": "Charlie Kirk", "revid": 1315259360, "parentid": 1315258906, "user": "Mikewem", "timestamp": "2025-10-05T18:14:21Z", "comment": "/* White Americans */His capitalized and in quotes use of the term here can reasonably be taken to be referring to the specific thing in France.", "tags": ["mobile edit", "mobile web edit", "advanced mobile edit"], "size": 214416}
{"rank": 68, "article": "Charlie Kirk", "revid": 1315258906, "parentid": 1315257444, "user": "Mikewem", "timestamp": "2025-10-05T18:11:37Z", "comment": "/* White Americans */I’m taking the uncapitalized use of great replacement to mean that he referred to the general idea, as opposed to the specific Great Replacement. The capitalized use specifically refers to France, whereas he is specifically referring to white rural America.", "tags": ["mobile edit", "mobile web edit", "advanced mobile edit"], "size": 214376}
{"rank": 68, "article": "Charlie Kirk", "revid": 1315257444, "parentid": 1315255970, "user": "Mikewem", "timestamp": "2025-10-05T18:03:12Z", "comment": "The Great Replacement article is about France. The white genocide article is about the US and says the term “replacement” has been used synonymously in the US with the white genocide conspiracy theory.", "tags": ["mobile edit", "mobile web edit", "advanced mobile edit"], "size": 214379}
{"rank": 68, "article": "Charlie Kirk", "revid": 1315255970, "parentid": 1315255381, "user": "Mikewem", "timestamp": "2025-10-05T17:53:49Z", "comment": "Our two sources for the icon claim both explicitly state that he achieved icon status during his lifetime, at some vaguely defined point. “To have become” reflects the vagueness in the sources. Assassination word and link are probably due for 1st paragraph, trying it here instead. This is the most common arrangement in other assassinated political figures pages.", "tags": ["mobile edit", "mobile web edit", "advanced mobile edit"], "size": 214349}
